arm64-darwin  clang builds the thrown `E(x)` straight into the exception object ([class.copy]/31's elision) and so has no copy that could throw, where cpp11 makes the temporary and copy-constructs the exception object from it, guarding that copy with ___cxa_free_exception - one symbol cpp11 has and clang does not, and the same difference new-header.nonames records. On x86_64-linux clang at -O0 makes the copy too and every name agrees; x86_64-windows agrees on every record.
x86_64-windows  one name, and cl is the authority on it: the ThrowInfo for a pointer to const is `_TI2CPEAD` in cl's object (the count, then C) and `_TIC2PEAD` in clang's (C first). cpp11 follows cl - names-vs-cl agrees on it - and the object is local to its file either way, the runtime matching a thrown type by its descriptor's name.
